# Every variable here is read by something. Two were removed in the D14 audit
# rather than left as instructions nobody follows:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME   never read; the product's name is a string in the
#                          message catalogues, where it can be translated.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN written for "Stage 3" and never wired. There is no
#                          error reporting and no analytics, deliberately:
#                          both want a deployment to send to, and there is
#                          none yet. See SECURITY.md.
#
# An example file that lists a variable the code ignores is worse than one
# that omits it -- somebody sets it, nothing happens, and they cannot tell
# whether the feature is off or broken.

# ── Where this deployment is served from ──
# Used for canonical URLs, the hreflang map, robots.txt and sitemap.xml -- all
# of which need an absolute origin and cannot guess one. **Set it at deploy
# time.** The fallback is localhost rather than a domain: a sitemap listing a
# hostname nobody has registered points at somebody else's site.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Backend API ──
# Same-origin path. In production nginx routes /api/* to Spring.
# In development next.config.ts rewrites it to API_PROXY_TARGET.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=/api/v1

# Rewrite target for local development. Server-side only -- never exposed.
API_PROXY_TARGET=http://localhost:8080

# ── Turnstile ──
# Site key for the sign-in form's challenge. Public by design; the secret is
# the backend's. Leave empty locally: with no secret configured the server has
# the challenge switched off, and the widget then draws nothing (B-050).
NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# ── Mocking ──
# "enabled" loads MSW handlers instead of calling the backend. Anything else
# (or removing it) talks to a real backend. It is also guarded on NODE_ENV,
# so a production build never carries the handlers whatever this says.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_MOCKING=enabled
